  "answer": "Found the bug on line 1602. Let me look at that region and also check `handler7`, which the test also exercises.\nThe file is mostly generated `handlerN` exports. Let me view the very end of the file where `shift` lives, and check the top of the file to confirm nothing else references off-by-one lookup.\nConfirmed: line 1602 (the file's last line) reads `return table[key + 1]` — the off-by-one. Fixing only that expression with a conditional edit:\nEdit applied. Now checking how tests are run and executing them:\nTests use `bun:test`. Running the suite:\nDone — the test passes. Summary:\n\n## The bug\n`src/registry.ts`, line 1602 (last line of the file):\n```ts\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key + 1] }\n```\n`shift` performed an off-by-one lookup: it returned the element *after* `key` instead of the element *at* `key` (and `undefined` for the last index).\n\n## The fix\nA single-expression change on that line, applied as a conditional edit against the file's SHA-256 fingerprint:\n```ts\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key] }\n```\n\n## What was not touched\n- The signature and export of `shift` are unchanged.\n- All 1,600 generated `handler0`–`handler1599` exports (plus `handler7`, which the test also asserts) are untouched.\n- `AGENTS.md`, `package.json`, and `test/oracle.test.ts` are untouched, and no configuration was added.\n\n## Test run\n```\nbun test v1.3.14\ntest/oracle.test.ts:\n(pass) lookup at the key\n 1 pass, 0 fail, 3 expect() calls\n```\nAll three assertions now hold: `shift([10,20,30], 0) === 10`, `shift([10,20,30], 2) === 30`, and `handler7(1) === 8`."
